heading out into the mediterranean sea  from the tunisian port city of gabis the  dive team's part of efforts to prevent  what activists warn could be an  environmental catastrophe  they were sent to inspect the hull of  the sunken tanker the zelo it went down  on saturday along with 750 tonnes of  diesel  the vessel flying the flag of equatorial  guinea was sailing from egypt to malta  its seven-man crew issued a distress  call on friday evening and sought  shelter from bad weather but the vessel  began taking on water the crew were  later safely brought to shore  tunisia's environment minister is  helping oversee the response she says  the situations under control  this is cargo of fuel oil or diesel it's  very different from oil in that fuel oil  is much less dangerous and tends to  evaporate fairly quickly we think the  hull is still watertight and there's no  leakage at the moment  authorities say barriers are being set  up to limit the spread of fuel and any  spillage would be mopped up  but local fishermen are angry their  livelihood relies on the waters where  the tanker is lying  the world wildlife fund says the area is  a fishing ground for 600 sailors with  the wider gulf providing jobs for around  34 000 others  we demand that specialists go down into  the sea and unload all the materials  inside if a leakage occurs we will not  have anything as we already only have a  little part of the sea left to live on  tunisia says several countries have  offered to help out to prevent any  pollution alexia o'brien al jazeera
 

